					"name": "Ork Kunnin'",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "This project's Talent (B89) for practical battlefield cunning: adds\nits level to Tactics, Traps, Camouflage and Observation, the same way\nthe Psyker Talent (Imperium/Traits/Psykers and the Warp.adq) adds to\nits own listed skills, per that file's house extension of Talent's\nnormal rules. An Ork Boy is not clever in the abstract (IQ 8 is IQ\n8), but a mob that has survived a hundred scraps has a bone-deep,\npractical instinct for where the enemy is weak, where the cover is,\nand when a trap will work — instinct this Talent represents directly\nrather than through raw IQ.\n\nLore: Ork tactics look like chaos from outside and are, by any human military-doctrine standard, chaos — and yet Ork mobs consistently find the gap in a defence, spring an ambush, or route around a strongpoint with an efficiency that surprises Imperial commanders who underestimate them. Codex background and Ork fiction play this as real, if inarticulate, battlefield cunning rather than dumb luck.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Psyker Talent uses for the same reason. Base entry costs 0 points at level 0; every template above Gretchin buys at least level 1 (10 points, included in the Ork Physiology package below).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "te5eFT53qIH29pfD6",
					"name": "WAAAGH! Gestalt",
					"local_notes": "Roughly ten or more fellow Orks fighting nearby, in earshot of the\nsame battle-roar, count as a WAAAGH!. While inside one:\n- Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp rolls get +2 (the conditional\n  bonus above).\n- Ork-built weapons and vehicles the character carries or crews have\n  their Malf number raised by 1 (fewer jams; e.g. Malf 14 becomes 15,\n  the Zzap Gun's 12 becomes 13). A lone Ork, or one with fewer than\n  ten fellows in earshot, gets no bonus. This is the mechanical hook\n  for \"Orks believe it, so it works, and it works better the more of\n  them believe it together.\"\n- A Weirdboy treats his own Warp Empowerment as one level higher (cap\n  +10) for the purpose of which known powers he may safely cast.\n  The Weirdboy template buys Empowerment 4 (cap 50), so Da Jump (60)\n  is castable only inside a WAAAGH!.\n\nLore: A lone Ork is dangerous; a mob of Orks worked up into a full WAAAGH! is a weaponised belief field — braver, louder, and (per Codex background's long-running joke about Ork technology) more likely to have its crude guns and rickety vehicles actually function, because enough Orks are collectively certain that they will. Weirdboyz in particular are consistently shown drawing directly on this collective psychic roar to power their biggest, most dangerous tricks; a Weirdboy isolated from a WAAAGH! is a much smaller threat.\n\nDesign: A conditional Fright Check/Perils bonus eyeballed against Basic's Fearlessness (2 points per level, so a flat +2 is 4 points, B55), restricted to a common-in-play situation (a mob of ten or more), which alone would price at about 2-3 points. The 5 points also buy two mechanical riders that are the point of the trait: an Ork-tech Malf +1 inside a WAAAGH!, and the Weirdboy's Empowerment +1. The Empowerment rider is not separately priced beyond that since it only changes which already-bought powers a Weirdboy may safely reach for.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "conditional_modifier",
							"situation": "to resist Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp, while fighting as part of a large mob of fellow Orks (a WAAAGH!)",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tIApC23PRlg6m2bTF",
					"name": "Fearlessness",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork has no fear to speak of: a lone Boy walks at a line of Marines because it looks like a good fight. It is the Gretchin who run. Below the Warboss's own extra levels, but enough that a Boy with Will 8 no longer fails a Fright Check as readily as a Guardsman.\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 3 (B55, 2/level, 6 points), a flat +3 to Fright Checks and Will rolls to resist fear and intimidation, on top of WAAAGH! Gestalt's conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TWJ-N3gZcEo41Ej4j",
			"name": "Gretchin Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Gretchin (Grots): the small, cowardly, expendable underclass of Ork society — servants, artillery crew and Runtherd-driven cannon fodder who share the same fungal biology as Boyz without the size, toughness or nerve. Consistent across Codex background and Black Library's Ork fiction, where a Grot's main survival strategy is staying near a bigger, meaner Ork and knowing when to vanish.\n\nDesign: -20 (Attribute Modifiers) + 0 (Fungal Physiology) + 10 (Resistant, Minor) + 5 (Grot Sneakiness) - 10 (Cowardice) - 5 (Social Stigma) - 1 (Squeals and Bolts) = -21 points of physiology alone — a genuinely weak template before skills, matching Gretchin's place as the Imperium's most disposable enemy rather than a threat in its own right; see the Gretchin template for the full (still very modest) character.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tyPTMkBJKCJ5nkCwR",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Gretchin)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: ST 8, DX 11, IQ 9, HT 10, HP 8, SM -1. Decreased ST -2 (B14, 10/level, -20) for ST 8; base HP tracks ST, so HP 8 needs no separate purchase. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 11 — sneaky and quick-fingered despite the weak frame. Decreased IQ -1 (B16, 20/level, -20) for IQ 9, slightly sharper than a Boy. HT 10 is baseline, no purchase. SM -1 is the framework's own figure for a Gretchin's small stature; it carries no independent point cost here since no ST is being bought above baseline for it to discount. -20+20-20 = -20 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": -20,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": -2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": -1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tfFO6S2T4Rk_C3yaV",
					"name": "Fungal Physiology (Spore-Born)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks are not born but grown: an Ork reproduces by releasing spores that seed fresh fungal growth wherever conditions allow (a mechanism shared with Gretchin, Squigs and the rest of the Ork ecosystem), has no biological sex, and needs neither a mate nor, ordinarily, a trained physician who isn't another Ork with a saw and a good attitude. This is consistent, well-established Codex background and is played straight rather than for shock across Black Library's Ork fiction — an Ork simply is an ambulatory fungal colony shaped like a green thug.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag. Every mechanical payoff of this biology (thick hide, extreme pain tolerance, fast healing, survivability of wounds that would kill outright, immunity to disease and poison) is priced separately above; this entry exists purely to document why an Ork needs no gender, no mate, and no bedside manner from anyone.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tJ1jOSIY7euf2kFae",
					"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins (Minor)",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Occasional: Disease and Toxins), Immunity.\n\nLore: Gretchin share the fungal-symbiote biology of full Orks, just a weaker dose of it — hardy compared to a human, sickly compared to a Boy.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Occasional: Disease and Toxins, Immunity tier — base 10, x1 for Immunity = 10 points, the same tier and math this project's Preomnor entry uses, well below a Boy's Common-tier 15.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tmaZR6FNakrwjjdb7",
					"name": "Grot Sneakiness",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "Adds its level to Stealth and Filch.\n\nLore: What a Gretchin lacks in strength it makes up in a genuine talent for not being noticed — slipping through a mob's legs to loot a battlefield, or vanishing the instant a Nob starts looking for someone to hit.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting two skills sits at the Small Talent rate (B90, 5 points/level, 1-2 skill bracket). Level 1, 5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t_GJkltSvv7J-sJ-a",
					"name": "Cowardice",
					"reference": "B129",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Gretchin are expendable and know it; they fight only when a bigger Ork is watching and run the instant that stops being true. Universally played for both pathos and slapstick across Ork fiction and Codex background alike.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Cowardice (B129), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tVRUN9fLFUmO12CMA",
					"name": "Social Stigma (Grot)",
					"reference": "B155",
					"local_notes": "Second-Class Citizen, within Ork society specifically.\n\nLore: Even by Ork standards a Gretchin is at the bottom of the pile — bossed, cuffed and worked by every Boy, Nob and Runtherd above it in the pecking order.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Social Stigma (Second-Class Citizen) (B155), -5 points, scoped to Ork society.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "t8Gu861lHL1s9Qj9L",
					"name": "Squeals and Bolts",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying flag only.\n\nLore: A Gretchin's first instinct under real pressure is noise, then distance.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TNp68a0lxabWgIwQg",
			"name": "Growth Lens: Nob",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Nob (short for \"Nobz,\" itself short for \"Nobber\") is a Boy who has survived enough fights, and eaten enough of the mob's best food, to keep growing well past where most Boyz stop. Nobs are the Ork mob's natural toughs, bodyguards and squad bosses — bigger, tougher and meaner, and canny enough to have picked up real tactical sense the hard way.\n\nDesign: 74 points, added to the Ork Physiology package's 186 for 260 points of physiology before skills. See the Ork Nob template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tHLvBEvg1ICOmCPHq",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Nob)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target over the Boy package: ST 26, DX 11 (unchanged), IQ 9, HT 14, HP 32, SM 0. Increased ST +4 (B14, 10/level, 40) for ST 26. Increased IQ +1 (B16, 20/level, 20) for IQ 9 — a Nob has learned a little sense surviving long enough to grow. Increased HT +1 (B15, 10/level, 10) for HT 14. Extra Hit Points +2 (B16, 2/level, 4) on top of the Boy package's own +4, for a combined extra +6 over the new ST-26 base of 26, giving HP 32. 40+20+10+4 = 74 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 74,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tH5W45dx4E9dq2Ng8",
					"name": "Big Nob (SM +1)",
					"local_notes": "Disabled by default; enable for an especially large, old Nob. 0-point flavour flag: this Nob's ST/HT/HP are already bought above, SM +1 just marks that this particular Nob is noticeably bigger even by Nob standards (and takes the usual SM combat penalties/bonuses that come with it), matching the framework's \"(+1 for big ones)\" note.\n\nLore: Some Nobs simply keep growing past the usual point, becoming hulking, unmistakable brutes even among their already-oversized kind.\n\nDesign: 0-point size flag; see notes.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0,
					"disabled": true,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TBQmUbdls2HWdNGud",
			"name": "Growth Lens: Warboss",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss is the biggest, meanest, canniest Ork in charge of a Waaagh — a Boy or Nob who kept fighting, kept winning, and kept growing until nothing short of a Space Marine or worse could reliably put him down. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! is the definitive Black Library portrait; current Codex: Orks background agrees a Warboss out-muscles a Space Marine on raw size and power alone.\n\nDesign: 166 (Attribute Modifiers) + 15 (Combat Reflexes) + 8 (Fearlessness 4) + 10 (Regeneration) + 6 (further Hard to Kill 3) + 15 (Reputation) + 15 (Charisma 3) + 10 (Basic Speed) + 20 (Wealth) = 265 points, added to the Ork Physiology package's 186 for 451 points of physiology and command presence before skills. See the Ork Warboss template, which lands the full character just under the low end of framework.md's Astartes point tier (550-800) — appropriate for a solid, competent Warboss; a legendary name like Ghazghkull himself would run well past this, into the framework's Heroes and Legends tier, and should be built as a bespoke NPC on top of this template rather than read off it directly.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t3xj9RMtxtk3aI5KP",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Warboss)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target over the Boy package: ST 32, DX 12, IQ 10, HT 15, HP 40, SM +1. Increased ST +10 (B14, 10/level, 100) for ST 32; per B18's Size and Strength Table a creature whose SM matches its actual bulk gets a cost break on ST, and SM +1 gives -10% on every level of ST bought, including the Boy package's +12: the Warboss's whole +22 costs 220 x 0.9 = 198, of which the Boy package already charged 120, so this lens adds 78 — the same discount this project's Custodian Attribute Modifiers takes for the same reason. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 12 — a Warboss hits harder and a little more precisely than a rank-and-file Boy, \"out-muscles a Space Marine\" more than out-fights one. Increased IQ +2 (B16, 20/level, 40) for IQ 10 — sharp enough to run a Waaagh's actual logistics of loot, grudges and turf, on top of Ork Kunnin's practical edge. Increased HT +2 (B15, 10/level, 20) for HT 15. Extra Hit Points +4 (B16, 2/level, 8) on top of the Boy package's own +4, for combined extra +8 over the new ST-32 base of 32, giving HP 40. SM +1 carries no cost beyond the ST discount already taken. 78+20+40+20+8 = 166 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 166,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 10
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tAwh0eaKXCPVpEjgu",
					"name": "Combat Reflexes",
					"reference": "B43",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss has been in more fights than any ten Boyz put together and simply doesn't flinch.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Combat Reflexes (B43), 15 points, as the Astartes/Custodes packages.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tdNOUxseNkaYMZy69",
					"name": "Fearlessness",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss is what other Orks are afraid of, not the other way around.\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 4 (B55, 2/level, 8 points), on top of the Boy package's Fearlessness 3 (7 levels in all) and WAAAGH! Gestalt's conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 4,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tjZwyl_eUm1Ktzcoq",
					"name": "Regeneration (Slow)",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Recovers 1 HP per 12 hours even without rest or first aid, on top of Rapid Healing.\n\nLore: The oldest, biggest Warbosses just don't stop knitting back together, a step beyond even an ordinary Boy's already-fast healing.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Slow) (B80, 10 points), stacking with the Boy package's Rapid Healing as a distinct passive-recovery benefit, per this project's Custodian Regeneration entry using the same two advantages together.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tycKNuyUOi1pC7Aiz",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "A further +3 to HT rolls to avoid death, stacking with the Boy package's Hard to Kill 3 for +6 total.\n\nLore: A Warboss dying on his feet is a running feature of Ork hero-tales, not an exception.\n\nDesign: A further Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points), stacking with the base package as GURPS allows multiple purchases of the same leveled advantage.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "ttapFRhtgQLAihjNL",
					"name": "Reputation (The Biggest and Baddest)",
					"reference": "B26",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss's reputation travels ahead of him — other mobs fall in behind him without a fight, and Imperial commanders who've faced a named Warboss before know exactly how bad the day is about to get.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B26), priced in line with this project's other eyeballed Reputation entries (Custodes' Shield-Captain Reputation, 15 points for +4) rather than a full frequency/scope calculation, per Basic's own guidance that Reputation costs are meant to be judged against comparable published examples.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "reaction_bonus",
							"situation": "from other Orks who recognise a Warboss's markings and retinue, and Fright Check bonus/penalty context for enemies who know his name",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t--p6qVTzpxRsaHMF",
					"name": "Charisma",
					"reference": "B41",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Ork society has no formal rank structure; a Warboss leads because every Ork in earshot wants to follow him, fears him, or both — Codex background is explicit that Warboss authority is pure force of personality (backed by a fist the size of a Rhino) rather than any chain of command.\n\nDesign: Charisma 3 (B41, 5/level, 15 points), used in place of a formal Rank trait since Ork command has no such structure.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t5nTFSVbsIwoMHJQx",
					"name": "Increased Basic Speed",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss's reflexes stay sharp long after his mob-mates' have dulled from age or drink.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Speed +0.50 (B17, 5 points per 0.25 level, 10 points).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_speed",
							"amount": 0.5
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tRvTKI4wniK67Ug4W",
					"name": "Wealth (Loot Hoard)",
					"reference": "B25",
					"local_notes": "Wealthy.\n\nLore: A successful Warboss accumulates a genuinely enormous pile of loot over a career — teef, scavenged tech, trophies — the Ork measure of status and the fuel for the next Waaagh's gear.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Wealth (Wealthy) (B25), 20 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 20
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "t8LGGEpLEHuk8s0bn",
			"name": "Mek Instinct",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Adds its level to Engineer (Ork Tech), Mechanic (Ork Tech), Armoury\n(Ork Tech) and Explosives, the same house extension of Talent's rules\nthe Psyker Talent uses. A Mek doesn't understand *why* a shoota's\ndakka-generator works any more than a Guardsman understands ballistic\nchemistry, but he has an instinctive, hands-on genius for building\nand fixing Ork machines that no amount of formal Mechanicus training\nwould improve on — and that formal training, notably, cannot\nreplicate: a captured Tech-Priest given a pile of scrap and a week\ndoes not produce a working Ork vehicle, but a Mek does.\n\nLore: Every Ork clan has Meks, and every Waaagh runs on what they weld together — walkers, trukks, shootas, and the occasional accidental superweapon. Codex background is explicit that Mek genius is instinctive rather than learned, and that Ork technology partly works because the whole Waaagh collectively believes it will (see WAAAGH! Gestalt); Ork fiction features Meks prominently as this exact kind of dangerous, half-mad genius (no single canonical figure).\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills, Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level), matching Ork Kunnin's own math for the same four-skill bracket. Base entry 0 points at level 0; the Mek template buys level 2 (20 points).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1,
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tkWDsTNOSFiD3n_DH",
			"name": "Painboy's Eye",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Adds its level to Surgery (Ork Methods), Physician (Ork Methods) and\nDiagnosis, the same house extension of Talent's rules the Psyker\nTalent uses. Ork medicine is brutal, fast and effective on Ork\npatients specifically — bone saws, magnets, and a total disregard for\nanaesthetic — and this Talent is what makes a Painboy's crude methods\nactually work on greenskin physiology as well as (or better than) a\ncareful human surgeon's would.\n\nLore: Painboyz (Doks) are Ork medics, and Ork medicine is not for the squeamish: replacement limbs bolted on with mismatched parts, \"brain surgery\" with a chisel, and patients who by any human medical standard should not have survived the treatment, let alone the original injury. Codex background and Black Library both play this completely straight — it works, on Orks, because a Painboy's tools and instincts are matched to Ork biology in a way finesse never would be.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting three skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket, same bracket as Ork Kunnin' and Mek Instinct with one skill to spare). Base entry 0 points at level 0; the Painboy template buys level 2 (20 points).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1,
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tu4VJuSKmigSJtIat",
			"name": "'Eadbanger",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A short-ranged psychic bludgeon that a Weirdboy throws at a single target's skull — described across Ork fiction as looking exactly like it sounds, a target's head rocking or worse under an unseen blow.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Crushing, 5/die, B61) with the standard Warp Sorcery/ Perils pair, net -30% (same derivation as this project's Assail), reflavoured for Ork psychic force rather than fine telekinetic control. Full modified cost: 5 x 0.7 = 3.5 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "my9UhYSgHayVinFwU",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mwA7TvKYIrXfBkOUL",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "tFk6ZFs-aZ7hjq6LF",
			"name": "Fists of Gork (an' Mork)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Channels raw WAAAGH! force into a Weirdboy's own fists (or whoever's fists he's pointing at), letting even a bare-handed blow crack armour plate — the source of the tabletop \"Warpath\"/\"Fists of Gork\" tradition of a Weirdboy briefly turning his mob into something that hits far harder than it should.\n\nDesign: Striking ST (5/level, B) with Warp Sorcery/Perils, net -30% (the same derivation this project's Sanctic Daemonology uses for Hammerhand). Full modified cost: 5 x 0.7 = 3.5 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mJGVhOOdau2QLN5tK",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mRBzTq_8r9qVLZncG",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "TR2B8AqK-_2lfEzC_",
			"name": "Warpath",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Warpath is the two halves of the same WAAAGH!-fuelled blessing a Weirdboy throws over his own mob before a charge: sudden strength and sudden speed, together. Tabletop and Codex background both describe Weirdboyz using exactly this kind of squad-wide buff in the moments before Orks hit a line.\n\nDesign: Grouped as GCS sums the children: 12 (Heroic Feats of Strength) + 6 (Warp Speed, Mob, at level 1) = 18 points at its default starting level.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tvN_GT6MX7hc0fL_O",
					"name": "Heroic Feats of Strength",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A howled WAAAGH! that fills a Weirdboy's whole mob with sudden, reckless strength for the charge.\n\nDesign: Basic's Heroic Feats of Strength (flat 10) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 10 x 1.2 = 12 points; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30) — the same build this project's Librarius: Might of Heroes uses.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mYqTjsARmuGuWUCaV",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mqTbbESMXaw-vjn3p",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mTKEbQm_HacAeFu07",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tOLwRkP6G4OEiC2me",
					"name": "Warp Speed (Mob)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The same burst quickens the whole mob's feet, turning a shambling charge into a genuine sprint.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Speed (5 points per +0.25) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 5 x 1.2 = 6 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mprTWJvrT6Zor38cH",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mKHNG5z3V6gPguIl8",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m6PIuTv_NE5dtUc7a",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tgwD4y2QvHdrEWOPR",
			"name": "Da Jump",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Weirdboy's signature and most feared trick: he grabs the nearest mob of Boyz, screams, and the whole lot vanish and reappear somewhere else on the battlefield an instant later — sometimes exactly where intended, sometimes scattered, occasionally (per the fiction's favourite running joke about Da Jump) several yards in the air. Weirdboyz and Da Jump-style warp effects recur in Ork fiction, played both as genuine tactical surprise and as slapstick; there is no single canonical mechanic.\n\nDesign: Built on Basic's own Warp advantage (B99, instant travel to any place you have seen/visualised, flat 100), matching this project's Gate of Infinity build, with Area Effect (+50%, B102, brings the whole mob) added on top, Preparation Required (-20%, a moment's howling concentration), Uncontrollable (Destructive, -30%, B116; the -10% Harmless version does not fit, since a mis-aimed mob is a real hazard: a Weirdboy can't always land the mob exactly where he meant to, the \"scatter\" of the tabletop, modelled honestly rather than smoothed over), Warp Sorcery (-20%) and the Severe Perils tier (-20%, a botched Jump is exactly the kind of interstellar-workings-adjacent disaster that tier exists for). Net: +50-20-30-20-20 = -40%. Full modified cost: 100 x 0.6 = 60 points — needing Warp Empowerment level 5 (cap 60), one more than a lone Weirdboy's Empowerment 4 (cap 50) reaches, so Da Jump is castable only inside a WAAAGH!, where WAAAGH! Gestalt raises his Empowerment to 5 (cap 60): the biggest trick in a Weirdboy's book needs the mob behind him.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mJHa7VDT0jpgvCF--",
					"name": "Area Effect",
					"cost_adj": "+50%"
				},
				{
					"id": "meDXJtKRS4mhVn7G9",
					"name": "Preparation Required",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mSPgG2u_0ZnLWbFve",
					"name": "Uncontrollable (Destructive)",
					"reference": "B116",
					"cost_adj": "-30%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mI_VvGDwIN2utLMtp",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mFukdZmdCk5uWQqt9",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp (Severe)",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 100
		},
		{
			"id": "ToKGAYN0XagrCqqbf",
			"name": "Lens: Stormboy Rokkit Pack",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Stormboyz are young, fast, fanatical Boyz who strap a crude, Mek-built rokkit pack to their backs and launch themselves at the enemy in short bounding flights: a spectacular, popular and genuinely dangerous way to get into a fight fast, since the packs are exactly as reliable as the rest of Ork technology. They are ordinary Boyz with a pack, not Kommando derivatives, so the lens belongs on the Boy template.\n\nDesign: Net 17 points (18 for the pack, -1 quirk); disabled by default so the base Boy stays a pure ground trooper. Enable it on a Boy (or any Ork template) to build a Stormboy.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tl3qj8BP_o9MaSUO4",
					"name": "Rokkit Pack (Short-Burst Flight)",
					"reference": "B56",
					"local_notes": "Flight (B56) for a short bounding flight: Air Move is twice Basic Move, for a few short bursts before the pack is spent, and the wearer cannot hover. A pack that malfunctions (Malf 14, 15 within a WAAAGH!) is spent at once and the landing is the GM's to complicate.\n\nLore: A single good burst from a rokkit pack throws a Stormboy well over the heads of the enemy line and down into it.\n\nDesign: Flight (B56, 40 points) with Cannot Hover (-15%, B56), Limited Use 3-4 uses (-20%, B112) and Accessibility, working pack only (-20%, B110). Net -55%, 40 x 0.45 = 18 points. Replaces an earlier Super Jump 1, which only doubled a leap and did not model a jump pack.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mvJ8n-NwxdwD2FyrJ",
							"name": "Cannot Hover",
							"reference": "B56",
							"cost_adj": "-15%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mLSWg_oacFiSYUvLj",
							"name": "Limited Use, 3-4 bursts per pack",
							"reference": "B112",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m31uHI9TmpRHAI0R2",
							"name": "Accessibility, only while wearing a working rokkit pack",
							"reference": "B110",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 40
				},
				{
					"id": "tnrMwLLHF5m9EmUlF",
					"name": "Rokkit Malfunctions",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying/GM-hazard flag only.\n\nLore: Rokkit packs are built by the same Meks and to the same standards as every other piece of Ork tech, which is to say: they work, up until the moment they spectacularly don't. A botched Stormboy landing is played for comedy about as often as the charge itself is played for menace.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9): a GM is free to call for an occasional bad landing on unlucky rolls, at no further point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "T9doWaSLSAFwcVTwo",
			"name": "Klan: Blood Axe",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Blood Axes study and copy human (and other) military doctrine — formations, camouflage, combined arms — and are consequently distrusted by every other Klan as not being proper Orks at all.\n\nDesign: Net 0 points, per-Klan flavour lens, matching the Guardsman template's regimental-lens pattern.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t694fqe3FDm4AnuXx",
					"name": "Human Tactics",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Blood Axe has actually studied how humans fight, and borrows what works.\n\nDesign: Perk (B9), +1 to Tactics.",
